   "content": "Thread Reader Share this page! × Post Share Email Enter URL or ID to Unroll × Unroll Thread You can paste full URL like: https://x.com/threadreaderapp/status/1644127596119195649 or just the ID like: 1644127596119195649 How to get URL link on X (Twitter) App On the Twitter thread, click on or icon on the bottom Click again on or Share Via icon Click on Copy Link to Tweet Paste it above and click \"Unroll Thread\"! More info at Twitter Help Ashpreet Bedi Subscribe @ashpreetbedi 22h • 6 tweets • 2 min read • Read on X Bookmark Gift Link Save as PDF Pro Tip for Agent Builders Most Agents fail at long-running, complex tasks (deep research, reporting, long-form analysis). If it only works 1/5 times, you can't build a product on it. Solution: Build workflows and give them to your Agents as tools. Learn more 👇 There are 2 big tradeoffs in today’s agentic systems: 1. We can get step-wise, deterministic execution (i.e. a workflow). This is more of an automation, it needs to be manually triggered with fixed inputs. This is about 80% of what businesses build today. 2. We can get free-form chatbot-like Agents that take text input, but these aren't deterministic in any way, and anything complex will just not work. By that I mean it won't work the same every time (I know people just select the 1 time it works for the demos). So how do we get both, free-form input and deterministic output on complex tasks? How do we build the \"deep-research\" like experience for Agents? Easy (well not easy, but its doable). Identify long-running tasks you want to solve: - Deep research - Run SQL + generate a report - Do market research before a call Turn each into a step-based workflow that works reliably. Then give this workflow as a tool to your Agent. Bonus: yield intermediate events from workflows and show them to the user. This builds transparency, trust, and a great UX. Thats it folks, that's how you add deterministic, long-running tasks to your Agents. If you found this useful, RT, follow, and ⭐️ Agno on github: agno.link/gh GitHub - agno-agi/agno: High-performance runtime for multi-agent systems. Build, run and manage secure multi-agent systems in your cloud. High-performance runtime for multi-agent systems. Build, run and manage secure multi-agent systems in your cloud. - agno-agi/agno https://agno.link/gh • • • Missing some Tweet in this thread? You can try to force a refresh Post Share Email Keep Current with Ashpreet Bedi Stay in touch and get notified when new unrolls are available from this author! Add to \"My Authors\" Read all threads This Thread may be Removed Anytime! Twitter may remove this content at anytime! Save it as PDF for later use! Save this thread as PDF Try unrolling a thread yourself! Follow @ThreadReaderApp to mention us! From a Twitter thread mention us with a keyword \"unroll\" @threadreaderapp unroll Practice here first or read more on our help page ! More from @ashpreetbedi Ashpreet Bedi @ashpreetbedi Apr 13 If you want to build good Agents, you need to know how to stitch together models, tools, instructions, reasoning, knowledge, storage and memory. Here's how I use them (link to code included) 👇 Firstly, what are Agents? \"Agents are AI programs that operate autonomously.\" That’s it, keep it simple. Overcomplicating this will only makes things harder. The \"brain\" of an Agent is the model, powering its ability to reason, act, and communicate effectively. Here's a basic Agent that calls the model API to generate a response. Agno provides a unified interface to 23+ model providers, so you can test different providers and switch models as needed. Read 11 tweets Ashpreet Bedi @ashpreetbedi Apr 11 🌶️ Hot take: The only way Autonomous Multi-Agent Systems work is by adding Reasoning & Agentic Context. I've tried it all, and here are my learnings👇 At @AgnoAgi we've been building multi-agent systems for almost 2 years using the handoff/transfer pattern that is becoming popular now. (Spoiler Alert: It doesnt work) Here's a video from over a year ago that demonstrates this: Ashpreet Bedi @ashpreetbedi · Follow Inspired by the fantastic work by @mattshumer_ , created an open-ended team of GPT-4 Journalists to write an article. Code: https:// dub.sh/journalist - Searcher: Finds the most relevant articles - Writer: Writes a draft of the article - Editor: Edits the draft Watch on X Matt Shumer @mattshumer_ Introducing `claude-journalist` The first Claude 3 journalist agent. Just provide a topic, and it will: - Search the web for articles/real-time details - Choose the best sources and read through them - Write a fantastic, *factual* article + edit it And it's open-source! 7:45 AM · Apr 17, 2024 139 Reply Copy link Read 9 replies There are two approaches to multi-agent systems: - Autonomous: A leader Agent orchestrates member Agents to achieve the task. The developer builds the Team & Agents but lets the leader Agent decide how to solve the task. This is 50% software engineering and 50% AI engineering. - Controlled: The developer explicitly defines the Teams, Agents, and workflow steps needed to accomplish the task. This requires substantial effort, 99% software engineering and 1% AI engineering. Because our clients demand reliability, we have traditionally guided them toward controlled workflows. It has been the only way to achieve consistent outputs from multi-agent systems. Read 11 tweets Ashpreet Bedi @ashpreetbedi Mar 27 It's time! Agno is now Generally Available 🚀 The simplest, fastest, full-featured library for building Agentic Systems is production-ready → 2 years, 2500 PRs, 22K ⭐️ → 1M+ new Agents every week → Built-in Memory, Knowledge, Reasoning & Tools Here's my open source journey In 2022, I left @AirbnbEng after 5 years, took some time off and started helping companies build AI products. When gpt-3.5 came out, RAG was all the rage, so that's what we built. We tried everything but nothing seemed to work. It always felt like I was fighting the \"framework of the month.\" The concepts of chains and prompt templates just didn't make sense - wasn't that what Python was for? Why did I need a framework for that? I was never a merchant of complexity, so I reverted to doing everything by hand, making API calls directly, chunking, scraping, loading vector databases. It was fun and the more AI products I built, I started encapsulating these utilities into classes. This was a healthy abstraction, maximum 1 layer deep and the goal was to expose the LLM APIs through a unified interface, not wrap them. We called these classes \"Assistants\" and they worked really, really well. Read 10 tweets Ashpreet Bedi @ashpreetbedi Oct 16, 2024 🚀 Say hello to the new & improved phidata 🚀 Build, ship, and monitor Agents with blazing-fast memory, knowledge, tools & reasoning 🔥 ⚡️ 70% faster memory & knowledge 🛠 100+ tools 🧠 Reasoning Agents 🤝 Multi-agent collaboration 📊 Built-in monitoring and Agent UI First a recap - phidata turns any LLM into an intelligent Agent with memory, knowledge, tools and reasoning ✨ Here's a simple finance agent Want to add reasoning to your Agents? Just set `reasoning=True` and the Agent will work through the problem step-by-step, validating and correcting as needed 🧠 Read 7 tweets Did Thread Reader help you today? Support us! We are indie developers! This site is made by just two indie developers on a laptop doing marketing, support and development! Read more about the story . Become a Premium Member ($3/month or $30/year) and get exclusive features! Become Premium Don't want to be a Premium member but still want to support us? Make a small donation by buying us coffee ($5) or help with server cost ($10) Donate via Paypal Or Donate anonymously using crypto! Ethereum 0xfe58350B80634f60Fa6Dc149a72b4DFbc17D341E copy Bitcoin 3ATGMxNzCUFzxpMCHL5sWSt4DVtS8UqXpi copy Thank you for your support! Follow Us! Post Share Send Email! × Email the whole thread instead of just a link! Separate emails with commas Message Send Thread As Email! Email Successfully Sent! Help | About | TOS | Privacy"
